<%# locals: (site_name:, page_title: nil, page_description: nil, fallback_image: nil) %>
<%# Open Graph and Twitter card tags — what a link to this page renders as when
    it is pasted into a chat or a social feed. All three apps need the same eight
    tags and differ only in what they call themselves, so the site name, the
    title and the description arrive as locals and the shape lives here.

    The canonical, description and JSON-LD tags stay in each app's layout. They
    are not card metadata, and brgen's city_copy_contract_test reads
    layouts/application.html.erb directly for pages.default_description and
    website_json_ld to prove no city renders another city's copy — a check this
    partial must not walk out from under.

    fallback_image is optional: pass it and every page gets a share thumbnail,
    omit it and only the pages that set content_for(:og_image) do. brgen passes
    its brand mark; amber and bsdports have never emitted an image tag and still
    do not. %>
<% custom_image = content_for(:og_image).presence %>
<% share_image = custom_image || fallback_image %>
<meta property="og:site_name" content="<%= site_name %>">
<meta property="og:title" content="<%= page_title || site_name %>">
<meta property="og:description" content="<%= page_description %>">
<meta property="og:url" content="<%= request.original_url %>">
<meta property="og:type" content="<%= content_for(:og_type).presence || "website" %>">
<%# summary_large_image only for a page that named its own image: the brand-mark
    fallback is a square icon and reads badly blown up to a wide card. %>
<meta name="twitter:card" content="<%= custom_image ? "summary_large_image" : "summary" %>">
<meta name="twitter:title" content="<%= page_title || site_name %>">
<meta name="twitter:description" content="<%= page_description %>">
<% if share_image %>
  <meta property="og:image" content="<%= share_image %>">
  <meta name="twitter:image" content="<%= share_image %>">
<% end %>
